From 4655653e99cab36b56a37589abf079a8127647ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 20:08:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] SOCK: listening sockets always reusable (SO_REUSEADDR),
 except on Windows (known issue 132)

After a simulator that had connections on a listening port exited, the
port could not be attached again for about a minute ("Sockets: bind error
98 - Address already in use"): the closed connections wait in TIME_WAIT
or FIN_WAIT2, and Linux lets a new listener bind meanwhile only when the
old sockets and the new one both have SO_REUSEADDR.  It was set only with
ATTACH -U, so -U helped only if the earlier simulator had used -U too.

sim_master_sock_ex now sets SO_REUSEADDR on every listening socket on
all systems but Windows (POSIX semantics: still never two listeners on
one address and port).  Windows keeps SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE unless -U.
HELP texts of sim_tmxr.c updated.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SuavtcpUxEC2vXhit3JaAg
---
 sim_sock.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 sim_tmxr.c | 16 ++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 23 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sim_sock.c b/sim_sock.c
index f50c63c9..02fd5997 100644
--- a/sim_sock.c
+++ b/sim_sock.c
@@ -23,6 +23,10 @@
    used in advertising or otherwise to promote the sale, use or other dealings
    in this Software without prior written authorization from Robert M Supnik.
 
+   28-Nov-26    Claude  Listening sockets always get SO_REUSEADDR, except on
+                        Windows: a port can be listened on again at once after
+                        a simulator with connections on it exited (before
+                        only with -U on both the old and the new listener)
    15-Oct-12    MP      Added definitions needed to detect possible tcp 
                         connect failures
    25-Sep-12    MP      Reworked for RFC3493 interfaces supporting IPv6 and IPv4
@@ -1073,6 +1077,15 @@ if (preferred->ai_family == AF_INET6) {
     sta = setsockopt (newsock, IPPROTO_IPV6, IPV6_V6ONLY, (char *)&off, sizeof(off));
     }
 #endif
+#if !defined (_WIN32)
+/* On POSIX systems SO_REUSEADDR only lets a listener bind while earlier
+   connections on the port are still shutting down (TIME_WAIT, FIN_WAIT2);
+   it never allows two listeners on the same address and port.  Linux
+   honours it only when the old sockets had it as well, so it is set on
+   every listening socket, not only with -U.  On Windows SO_REUSEADDR
+   would let a second program share a port in use: there it stays -U. */
+opt_flags |= SIM_SOCK_OPT_REUSEADDR;
+#endif
 if (opt_flags & SIM_SOCK_OPT_REUSEADDR) {
     int on = 1;
 
